    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d462">

                <pb facs="0452" n="440" sameAs="frus:frus1944v05/pg440"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.51A/12–144: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Iran</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Morris</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-12-06T19:00:00-04:00">December 6, 1944—7
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>712. Dept concurs in position taken in your conversation with the Prime Minister,
                    as reported in urtel 890, December 1. Dept believes that, in view of intervening
                    developments you acted wisely in refraining from informing Iranian authorities
                    of the purport of our 679, November 20.<anchor n="45" xml:id="d462-f1"/> The
                    intent of that message was not to suggest Gresham as a possible successor to
                    Millspaugh but merely to express concurrence in his continued service in Iran as
                    an American who has demonstrated good performance and cooperation with the
                    Iranians.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
